[s0l0!-header][h1]The default Keyword[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

Often there will be no match, but we still need the program to output something...for this we use the [b]default [/b]keyword, which specifies the code to run if there’s no case match.

[s0l0!-header][b]Like this:[/b][/s0l0!-header][code format="js" codeId="666"]var color ="[b]yellow[/b]";
switch(color) {
   case "blue": 
     document.write("This is blue.");
     break;
   case "red": 
     document.write("This is red.");
     break;
   case "green": 
     document.write("This is green."); 
     break;
   case "orange": 
      document.write("This is orange."); 
      break;
   [b]default[/b]: 
      document.write("Color not found.");
}
[/code]
[note][b]Heads up![/b]
The default block can be omitted, if there is no need to handle the case when no match is found.
[/note]

